WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.541 --> 00:00:10.977
Hi, I’m David Baker from the University of Washington.

00:00:10.978 --> 00:00:15.748
Today, I’m gonna talk about crowdsourcing protein folding.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:19.318
So, as I described in my iBio seminars,

00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:22.755
my group is working on predicting the structures

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:24.490
of naturally occurring proteins

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:27.860
and designing brand new protein structures.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:32.498
And these calculations are very computationally intensive,

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:35.535
so a number of years ago

00:00:35.536 --> 00:00:37.970
we had filled up every nook and cranny of my lab,

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.505
and the hallway next door,

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:42.708
with computer boxes

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:45.378
and were getting into trouble because we were heating up the building

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:47.847
and it was very expensive,

00:00:47.848 --> 00:00:49.782
and we were… still didn’t have enough computing power

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:52.685
to accurately predict protein structures

00:00:52.686 --> 00:00:55.555
or to design new proteins.

00:00:55.556 --> 00:00:59.325
So, we started a project called Rosetta@Home,

00:00:59.326 --> 00:01:02.995
which you can look up on the web,

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:04.864
and please join.

00:01:04.865 --> 00:01:09.969
If you sign up for Rosetta@Home, you will…

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.739
when you’re not using your computer, a screensaver will appear,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.209
and what that screensaver will show you

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.144
will be the course of a calculation,

00:01:18.145 --> 00:01:20.279
a protein structure prediction calculation

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.148
or a protein design calculation,

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:24.116
that we’ve sent you.

00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:25.918
And so, for every challenge that we have,

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.954
whether it’s predicting a protein structure

00:01:27.955 --> 00:01:30.022
or designing a new protein,

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:32.792
we send the sequence out to…

00:01:32.793 --> 00:01:36.262
out to you, if you become a Rosetta@Home volunteer,

00:01:36.263 --> 00:01:39.699
and then Rosetta@Home volunteers

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.134
send us back automatically the solutions.

00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:45.471
And those are… that’s really the workhorse of computation

00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:48.474
in my research group.

00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:52.879
And currently there are over 350,000 Rosetta@Home volunteers,

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.649
and I hope there’ll be one more after you listen to video.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.551
And that, again, is really making…

00:01:58.552 --> 00:02:01.220
enabling the research that we’re doing.

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:03.489
So, as part of Rosetta@Home,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.259
we give feedback on the message boards

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.195
for the project about the…

00:02:09.196 --> 00:02:13.533
about the progress of the research,

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:18.838
and participants write in and ask questions,

00:02:18.839 --> 00:02:21.607
and we try to answer those questions.

00:02:21.608 --> 00:02:24.877
I try and do it myself,

00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:26.746
but I don’t do it probably as often as I should…

00:02:26.747 --> 00:02:28.915
but… so, we have a dialogue with participants,

00:02:28.916 --> 00:02:31.050
which has really been fun,

00:02:31.051 --> 00:02:33.953
and has given me and others in my group

00:02:33.954 --> 00:02:36.255
an opportunity to describe what we do to the general public.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:39.492
Well, a number of years ago, Rosetta@Home volunteers wrote in and said,

00:02:39.493 --> 00:02:41.527
we’re watching the screensaver,

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:45.431
we’re seeing the… we see the protein folding up,

00:02:45.432 --> 00:02:49.936
but we’ve been watching it for long enough to think that the prot…

00:02:49.937 --> 00:02:52.171
the computer is really not doing as well as it could.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:54.307
If only I could get in there

00:02:54.308 --> 00:02:56.409
and sort of guide the protein to fold up and…

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:59.011
I think I could get it to a better job…

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.348
a better job finding the lowest energy state.

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:05.151
So, this inspired us to team up with

00:03:05.152 --> 00:03:09.422
a group in the Computer Science Department at the University of Washington

00:03:09.423 --> 00:03:11.891
to develop a… basically, an interactive version of Rosetta@Home

00:03:11.892 --> 00:03:13.793
called FoldIt.

00:03:13.794 --> 00:03:15.995
Now, in FoldIt…

00:03:15.996 --> 00:03:18.631
there’s a screenshot of FoldIt shown here.

00:03:18.632 --> 00:03:21.434
You see the protein molecule that umm…

00:03:21.435 --> 00:03:26.506
that we call the… each prob… of FoldIt,

00:03:26.507 --> 00:03:28.541
each problem we’re working on we frame as a puzzle for FoldIt.

00:03:28.542 --> 00:03:32.612
So, here you see the protein chain,

00:03:32.613 --> 00:03:34.780
and one of the big innovations near the beginning,

00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:38.351
as I described in my iBio seminar,

00:03:38.352 --> 00:03:41.454
the… proteins fold to their lowest energy states,

00:03:41.455 --> 00:03:45.558
but it wasn’t a very good game when you were competing to get a lower score.

00:03:45.559 --> 00:03:48.928
So, we took the energy and multiplied it by -100,

00:03:48.929 --> 00:03:52.031
so now the goal was to get a higher end… higher score…

00:03:52.032 --> 00:03:54.734
higher scores are better, and it’s a com…

00:03:54.735 --> 00:03:57.803
FoldIt is a competitive game, so you’re moving the protein around.

00:03:57.804 --> 00:03:59.238
FoldIt computes the energy.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:00.973
It’s basically an interface to Rosetta@Home,

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:03.009
so it’s computing the energy in the same way

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.644
that Rosetta does,

00:04:04.645 --> 00:04:07.046
but rather than having the computer randomly search around,

00:04:07.047 --> 00:04:08.915
you as a FoldIt player

00:04:08.916 --> 00:04:12.685
are doing the search by moving the protein around.

00:04:12.686 --> 00:04:14.487
Now, you don’t have to do it all by hand.

00:04:14.488 --> 00:04:18.324
There are tools on the bottom left…

00:04:18.325 --> 00:04:20.059
that are shown on the bottom left,

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.595
which are basically Rosetta algorithms

00:04:22.596 --> 00:04:24.263
which you can call to help you,

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:27.400
which for example will find the nearest local minimum

00:04:27.401 --> 00:04:29.836
or search for a different conformation of the side chains,

00:04:29.837 --> 00:04:33.239
which are these stick-like structures coming off the backbone.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:40.213
And the… Rosetta@Home only has access to the… to the…

00:04:40.214 --> 00:04:44.784
these automated algorithms, whereas with FoldIt…

00:04:44.785 --> 00:04:47.520
a FoldIt player… and you can go download FoldIt

00:04:47.521 --> 00:04:49.488
right after you finish watching this…

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:53.192
and when you do this, you’ll use a combination of

00:04:53.193 --> 00:04:55.061
your own intuition about where the low energy…

00:04:55.062 --> 00:04:57.296
lowest energy state might lie

00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:01.801
and these automated methods.

00:05:01.802 --> 00:05:03.870
Now, this is… that…

00:05:03.871 --> 00:05:06.939
what I’ve described so far is for a protein structure prediction problem,

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.575
but… but again, as I described in my iBio seminar,

00:05:09.576 --> 00:05:12.044
we’re very excited now about designing

00:05:12.045 --> 00:05:14.580
brand new proteins that never existed before

00:05:14.581 --> 00:05:16.516
to address current day challenges.

00:05:16.517 --> 00:05:20.152
And FoldIt players are indeed now designing new proteins.

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.722
So, in design puzzles,

00:05:22.723 --> 00:05:24.757
you can change not only the… where…

00:05:24.758 --> 00:05:26.792
the geometry of the protein, where the backbone is,

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:28.861
but you can change what amino acid is where —

00:05:28.862 --> 00:05:31.430
you can change the sidechains.

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:35.535
And we’ve… I’ve been really amazed

00:05:35.536 --> 00:05:37.870
at the wonderful things that FoldIt players

00:05:37.871 --> 00:05:39.472
have done over the years,

00:05:39.473 --> 00:05:42.675
and I’ll give you some highlights in the remainder of my talk.

00:05:42.676 --> 00:05:45.077
And the really interesting… one of the…

00:05:45.078 --> 00:05:47.680
the really interesting thing is that FoldIt players

00:05:47.681 --> 00:05:49.649
have made new scientific discoveries,

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.218
and I’ll describe several of these.

00:05:52.219 --> 00:05:56.255
The first discovery concerns determining the structure

00:05:56.256 --> 00:05:59.926
of a protein that’s from a retrovirus protease that’s…

00:05:59.927 --> 00:06:04.063
a protein… a retrovirus that’s related to HIV.

00:06:04.064 --> 00:06:06.899
This is a case where… the experimentalists

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.402
had collected experimental data

00:06:09.403 --> 00:06:12.138
but weren’t able to take that experimental data

00:06:12.139 --> 00:06:13.973
and solve the structure

00:06:13.974 --> 00:06:16.709
because they were missing some key pieces of information.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.111
So, we set this up as a FoldIt puzzle

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:22.415
and the puzzle started with a model

00:06:22.416 --> 00:06:24.717
which we knew was in the ballpark

00:06:24.718 --> 00:06:26.752
but certainly wasn’t the correct structure,

00:06:26.753 --> 00:06:29.121
and that’s shown in red on the left.

00:06:29.122 --> 00:06:31.524
And FoldIt players formed teams,

00:06:31.525 --> 00:06:33.993
and the teams…

00:06:33.994 --> 00:06:35.862
they collaborate very closely together

00:06:35.863 --> 00:06:38.197
within teams and compete with other teams

00:06:38.198 --> 00:06:40.533
in a friendly sort of way.

00:06:40.534 --> 00:06:42.635
So, these are… this is the result of a team,

00:06:42.636 --> 00:06:45.371
and I’m gonna show you the results of three of the teammates.

00:06:45.372 --> 00:06:47.707
So, the first teammate, named spvincent,

00:06:47.708 --> 00:06:51.143
took the red model

00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:53.079
and was able to find a lower energy structure,

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:54.680
which is shown in yellow.

00:06:54.681 --> 00:06:58.251
Now, in these diagrams, blue is the actual…

00:06:58.252 --> 00:07:01.053
the punchline is gonna be that FoldIt players solved the structure,

00:07:01.054 --> 00:07:02.955
and that structure is shown in blue.

00:07:02.956 --> 00:07:05.391
Of course, no one — we, neither…

00:07:05.392 --> 00:07:07.793
we, nor the FoldIt players — knew what it was at the time they were doing this.

00:07:07.794 --> 00:07:12.865
So, spvincent took the red model, got the yellow model, which was closer,

00:07:12.866 --> 00:07:15.768
and then handed it off to his teammate, grabhorn,

00:07:15.769 --> 00:07:19.639
and grabhorn produced the magenta structure,

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:22.141
and then finally grabhorn passed it off to mimi,

00:07:22.142 --> 00:07:24.644
and mimi ran the final lap of the relay.

00:07:24.645 --> 00:07:29.081
And you can see that mimi’s green structure

00:07:29.082 --> 00:07:32.285
is much closer to the actual structure, in blue,

00:07:32.286 --> 00:07:34.787
then was the starting structure

00:07:34.788 --> 00:07:36.689
that’s in red on the far left.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.491
And when we got mimi’s structure back,

00:07:38.492 --> 00:07:40.860
we could see immediately that had to be the correct answer

00:07:40.861 --> 00:07:43.296
because it fit beautifully the experimental data.

00:07:43.297 --> 00:07:46.899
So, the data weren’t sufficient to determine what the structure is,

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:48.868
but they were enough… they were like a fingerprint…

00:07:48.869 --> 00:07:51.037
you couldn’t tell what the person looked like from the fingerprint,

00:07:51.038 --> 00:07:53.139
but you could tell it was the right person

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:54.874
when you had the fingerprint.

00:07:54.875 --> 00:07:56.375
So, it’s just like that with the structure.

00:07:56.376 --> 00:07:58.945
mimi’s structure fit the experimental data beautifully

00:07:58.946 --> 00:08:01.981
and it was very, very exciting.

00:08:01.982 --> 00:08:04.483
The second example I want to give you

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:06.586
is show you that FoldIt players

00:08:06.587 --> 00:08:08.721
can actually come up with new algorithms.

00:08:08.722 --> 00:08:13.092
So, I told you that there were these automated Rosetta algorithms —

00:08:13.093 --> 00:08:16.028
that there were these buttons that you could click —

00:08:16.029 --> 00:08:18.164
and FoldIt players…

00:08:18.165 --> 00:08:21.100
we gave the FoldIt players the ability to create what we call recipes,

00:08:21.101 --> 00:08:23.202
where… which are different combinations

00:08:23.203 --> 00:08:26.973
of these automated algorithms.

00:08:26.974 --> 00:08:29.575
And these recipes became very popular in the community

00:08:29.576 --> 00:08:33.179
and many of them got to be very sophisticated.

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:36.415
This plot shows the time in weeks

00:08:36.416 --> 00:08:39.685
from when we first released the capability to…

00:08:39.686 --> 00:08:43.389
to make recipes.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:47.493
Each vertical bar is the number of uses of a recipe

00:08:47.494 --> 00:08:49.896
during that week,

00:08:49.897 --> 00:08:52.732
and the different colors or shades

00:08:52.733 --> 00:08:56.502
represent how many times each recipe was used.

00:08:56.503 --> 00:08:59.839
So, some FoldIt players are very public-spirited

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.709
and they make their recipes generally available to the community,

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.912
and others like to keep them for themselves.

00:09:05.913 --> 00:09:08.247
So, as you’d expect, the people who share get more uses.

00:09:08.248 --> 00:09:11.250
And we noticed that in particular there were two recipes,

00:09:11.251 --> 00:09:14.253
the one shown in red and the ones shown in green on this slide,

00:09:14.254 --> 00:09:17.857
that were really becoming very, very popular in the community.

00:09:17.858 --> 00:09:21.394
So, we were curious about what these were.

00:09:21.395 --> 00:09:24.063
And I’m not gonna go through the details,

00:09:24.064 --> 00:09:26.866
but the actual… the actual Rosetta…

00:09:26.867 --> 00:09:30.636
the actual recipe for one of the most popular,

00:09:30.637 --> 00:09:33.239
called Blue Fuse, is shown in the box.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.975
And what we’re really stunned by

00:09:35.976 --> 00:09:38.277
is that this Blue Fuse algorithm,

00:09:38.278 --> 00:09:40.847
which was taking over the population,

00:09:40.848 --> 00:09:42.949
was very, very similar to an algorithm

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:45.418
that we had been developing in the lab for this…

00:09:45.419 --> 00:09:48.387
for the same problem, for protein structure prediction.

00:09:48.388 --> 00:09:50.623
And I won’t go through the details of what it is,

00:09:50.624 --> 00:09:52.758
but let’s just… but the colors

00:09:52.759 --> 00:09:55.361
— the yellow, blue, and red —

00:09:55.362 --> 00:09:57.630
are different operations.

00:09:57.631 --> 00:09:59.932
And the algorithm we’d developed in the lab,

00:09:59.933 --> 00:10:02.768
which is shown in the middle… the middle column, has…

00:10:02.769 --> 00:10:05.471
you see this cycling between yellow, blue, and red,

00:10:05.472 --> 00:10:08.441
yellow, blue, and red,

00:10:08.442 --> 00:10:10.943
and what was… and we had never tried that before,

00:10:10.944 --> 00:10:11.210
and it worked really, really well.

00:10:11.211 --> 00:10:14.213
We were very excited about it.

00:10:14.214 --> 00:10:17.083
And then we found that in the Blue Fuse algorithm that the players had developed,

00:10:17.084 --> 00:10:20.086
it was very similar, alternating between these different operations.

00:10:20.087 --> 00:10:22.555
And then we got… it was kind of sobering to find

00:10:22.556 --> 00:10:23.723
that in the context of the game,

00:10:23.724 --> 00:10:26.025
this Blue Fuse algorithm was actually better

00:10:26.026 --> 00:10:29.562
than what we had developed as professional scientists in the lab.

00:10:29.563 --> 00:10:33.165
So, the final example concerns a protein design.

00:10:33.166 --> 00:10:36.435
And we have for many years

00:10:36.436 --> 00:10:39.605
been designing new enzyme catalysts in the lab,

00:10:39.606 --> 00:10:42.608
designing new proteins to catalyze reactions

00:10:42.609 --> 00:10:45.578
which aren’t catalyzed by naturally occurring proteins.

00:10:45.579 --> 00:10:48.648
And we do this by designing a protein,

00:10:48.649 --> 00:10:51.384
using the methods described in my iBio seminar,

00:10:51.385 --> 00:10:54.287
that holds the constituent chemicals

00:10:54.288 --> 00:10:57.557
in just the right orientation for the chemical reaction to occur.

00:10:57.558 --> 00:11:00.493
And when we make these proteins in the lab…

00:11:00.494 --> 00:11:03.863
when they’re successful, what we find is

00:11:03.864 --> 00:11:06.299
they actually catalyze the chemical reaction,

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.835
and that’s shown on the panel on the right here.

00:11:08.836 --> 00:11:11.537
But what’s not so good about these designed catalysts

00:11:11.538 --> 00:11:15.007
is the units on the x-axis.

00:11:15.008 --> 00:11:17.743
Rather than seconds, which is what a naturally occurring enzyme

00:11:17.744 --> 00:11:20.446
would take to catalyze a reaction,

00:11:20.447 --> 00:11:22.181
this enzyme is a very bad one —

00:11:22.182 --> 00:11:24.383
it takes hours to catalyze the chemical reaction.

00:11:24.384 --> 00:11:27.220
So, when we looked at this design,

00:11:27.221 --> 00:11:30.089
and we looked at the model of it, we saw that…

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:32.592
it’s sort of obvious what the problem is.

00:11:32.593 --> 00:11:35.728
The two chemicals are shown in these full…

00:11:35.729 --> 00:11:38.598
these ball-like figures in the middle.

00:11:38.599 --> 00:11:41.234
And one of them is… the lower one is quite small,

00:11:41.235 --> 00:11:42.702
and this designed catalyst does not

00:11:42.703 --> 00:11:45.538
really hold onto these very extensively.

00:11:45.539 --> 00:11:48.140
There’s not really a deep pocket that they fit into,

00:11:48.141 --> 00:11:51.911
and so these chemicals really don’t bind very strongly

00:11:51.912 --> 00:11:54.547
to the designed enzyme.

00:11:54.548 --> 00:11:57.116
Now, there are these loops of the pro…

00:11:57.117 --> 00:12:00.119
of the design that are coming out towards you that are in orange,

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.722
and it sort of seems like you might be able to change them

00:12:02.723 --> 00:12:04.690
in some way to bind those substrates, those small molecules, better,

00:12:04.691 --> 00:12:07.627
but it’s not really obvious to do… how to do that,

00:12:07.628 --> 00:12:09.962
and you certainly couldn’t tell a computer to re…

00:12:09.963 --> 00:12:11.764
to remodel the protein to bind the substrates better,

00:12:11.765 --> 00:12:13.599
those small molecules.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.202
But you can tell people that, so we asked the FoldIt community to do that,

00:12:16.203 --> 00:12:19.338
and they came up with this solution shown in purple here,

00:12:19.339 --> 00:12:21.474
where they built this whole new part of the protein

00:12:21.475 --> 00:12:24.143
to come over and bind those small molecules better.

00:12:24.144 --> 00:12:26.879
And what was really amazing when we went

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.048
and made this protein in the lab

00:12:29.049 --> 00:12:31.517
is that it actually catalyzed the reaction

00:12:31.518 --> 00:12:35.321
very, very much more rapidly — 20-fold more rapidly

00:12:35.322 --> 00:12:37.924
than the starting design.

00:12:37.925 --> 00:12:40.526
So, FoldIt players had figured out how to improve this design

00:12:40.527 --> 00:12:43.629
that we had made on the computer.

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:46.199
And when the crystal structure was solved

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:48.734
in Barry Stoddard’s lab, of their design,

00:12:48.735 --> 00:12:52.338
it turned out that it really folded up the way they predicted it to.

00:12:52.339 --> 00:12:54.574
And you can see… the design is in purple —

00:12:54.575 --> 00:12:56.943
that’s what the FoldIt players came up with —

00:12:56.944 --> 00:12:59.579
and the crystal structure is in gold.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:02.014
And again, it is very, very close.

00:13:02.015 --> 00:13:04.784
What FoldIt players now have done very recently

00:13:04.785 --> 00:13:07.119
I can call protein art.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.956
It’s very beautiful.

00:13:09.957 --> 00:13:12.425
In this… in this case, what they’ve done

00:13:12.426 --> 00:13:15.027
is taken an extended protein chain and…

00:13:15.028 --> 00:13:17.296
with no amino acid sequence on it,

00:13:17.297 --> 00:13:19.599
and here the challenge is to fold it up and to put an amino acid sequence on it

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.668
so that it interacts in a very low energy way

00:13:22.669 --> 00:13:25.471
with other identical copies of itself.

00:13:25.472 --> 00:13:28.441
And so, these are very beautiful structures,

00:13:28.442 --> 00:13:31.043
some of which resemble proteins which occur in nature

00:13:31.044 --> 00:13:33.980
and others which look nothing like that.

00:13:33.981 --> 00:13:35.581
For example, the one in the top left…

00:13:35.582 --> 00:13:38.451
there’s no protein that looks anything like this.

00:13:38.452 --> 00:13:41.187
So, we’re currently testing these brand-new proteins in the lab

00:13:41.188 --> 00:13:44.457
and we’re very excited to see what the results will be.

00:13:44.458 --> 00:13:46.993
And I’ve been very fortunate, again,

00:13:46.994 --> 00:13:50.229
to have really talented collaborators,

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:53.165
both in the Biochemistry Department and in the Computer Science Department,

00:13:53.166 --> 00:13:56.068
who are responsible for FoldIt.

00:13:56.069 --> 00:13:58.738
And most importantly, I want to thank the FoldIt players,

00:13:58.739 --> 00:14:01.674
who are really opening up this whole new area of scientific inquiry

00:14:01.675 --> 00:14:05.278
and demonstrating that there’s kind of been a…

00:14:05.279 --> 00:14:08.114
that these distinctions between scientists and the public, I think,

00:14:08.115 --> 00:14:11.350
are a little bit artificial,

00:14:11.351 --> 00:14:14.520
and that, really, anyone who’s interested

00:14:14.521 --> 00:14:17.223
can become a participating… can participate in scientific research.

00:14:17.224 --> 00:14:41.849
Thank you for your attention.

